<h2 id="one">One mention, and everything we would record about it</h2>
<p>Before any counting, here is one real line, from Arakhin 6a, as two editions print it.</p>
<div class="rec"><dl>
<dt>One edition prints</dt><dd><bdi class="he">אמר רבי אילא אמר רבי יוחנן לא קשיא</bdi></dd>
<dt>The other prints</dt><dd><bdi class="he">א״ר אילא א״ר יוחנן לא קשיא</bdi></dd>
<dt>Names we find</dt><dd><bdi class="he">רבי אילא</bdi> and <bdi class="he">רבי יוחנן</bdi>. At this point they are strings, not people.</dd>
<dt>What joins them</dt><dd>&ldquo;X said, Y said&rdquo;: Rabbi Ila is passing on a teaching of Rabbi Yochanan.
So the name Ila sits later in time than the name Yochanan. This is the strongest kind of link we have.</dd>
<dt>The wording</dt><dd>&ldquo;Said X&rdquo; with the verb first. That is how the Talmud introduces later sages.
Early teachers get &ldquo;X says&rdquo;. So both names here are later sages.</dd>
<dt>What the editions show</dt><dd>One spells out &ldquo;Rabbi&rdquo;. The other writes the short form. Every time an
editor opened up a short form, the editor decided who was meant.</dd>
<dt>What it does not say</dt><dd>Which Rabbi Ila. Which Rabbi Yochanan. That the two ever met.</dd>
<dt>What our app does today</dt><dd>It maps <bdi class="he">רבי אילא</bdi> to one entry in our list and shows that man everywhere.
I shipped that. It is one of the things this page says to change.</dd>
</dl></div>

<h2 id="review">What holds up from the work so far, and what does not</h2>
<p>I went back over everything from this project with a harder eye. The short version: the
software changes mostly hold. The research numbers mostly do not, yet. They point the right way,
but I reported them with more confidence than they had earned.</p>

<h3>I read 40 of my own results by hand</h3>
<figure>{{FIG_AUDIT}}</figure>
<p>The repair was meant to stop a father&rsquo;s name swallowing the next word. Instead it cut any
name whose given name starts with one of seven letters. Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Shimon both became
plain &ldquo;Rabbi&rdquo;. That hit {{TRUNC}} of the links, about {{TRUNC_PCT}}. The fifth error read
&ldquo;Master, what did Shmuel say&rdquo; as a sage called Mar Mai. None of this was caught by a test,
because there are no tests. I found it by reading.</p>

<h3>Most &ldquo;names seen once&rdquo; are not names</h3>
<figure>{{FIG_SINGLE}}</figure>
<p>The four most common one-word names in the Talmud are Rav, Rabbi, Rava and Rabbah. My name
finder never sees them alone. It glues them to whatever word comes next, so &ldquo;Rava, following
his own view&rdquo; becomes a sage. This is the biggest open problem in finding names, and it has
to be solved before any count of people means anything.</p>

