<p><strong>How I worked, not only what I found.</strong> I merged two changes while the checks were
red. I raised a false alarm about live behaviour. I called a name &ldquo;clear&rdquo; from one search
result. Three times I started seven helpers at once and ran into a spending cap. Still not done
from the earlier list: the four other uses for the judging model, the look at new reader
components, and rebuilding the page index.</p>

<h2 id="rules">Five rules the mistakes taught us</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>A name is a string until proven otherwise.</strong> Nothing gets attached to a person until the
evidence for that person has been weighed.</li>
<li><strong>The Talmud is an edited book, not a record of events.</strong> &ldquo;Said to&rdquo; does not mean
they met. &ldquo;And some say&rdquo; is about where a teaching came from. A visit may be a story. Every
phrase is a clue with a weight, and the weight has to be measured.</li>
<li><strong>Editions are opinions.</strong> Editors opened up short forms and corrected names. Where two
editions differ, somebody disagreed about who it was.</li>
<li><strong>Every outside record is a claim to test.</strong> Our own list, Sefaria&rsquo;s tags, the
biographical dictionaries. None of them goes into the graph. All of them get scored against it
at the end.</li>
<li><strong>No number without a hand check.</strong> Every figure I report comes with a sample somebody
read, and &ldquo;we cannot tell&rdquo; is an allowed answer.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="sources">The sources we have</h2>
<p>All of it is Hebrew and Aramaic, taken from Sefaria with the edition, link, date and licence
recorded for each piece. No translation is used anywhere. A translator has already decided who
is who.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Body of text</th><th>Size</th><th>Editions</th><th>How it is divided</th><th>What it adds</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>{{SRC_ROWS}}</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>What this table is and is not.</strong> I checked the editions on one work from each body of
text, not on all of them. Step 2 of the plan counts them properly. The Mishnah is the pleasant
surprise: one of its three editions follows the Kaufmann manuscript, which is a truly separate
witness. The Bavli is the disappointment: both its editions come down from the same Vilna
printing, so when they agree it proves little.</p>
<p><strong>What we do not have.</strong> The great manuscripts of the Bavli exist as transcriptions
elsewhere, in collections I have not checked access to. I will not plan around them until I have
opened them.</p>
<p><strong>Kept apart until the end:</strong> our own list of {{ENTRIES}} entries, of which {{NOERA}} have no
era at all; Sefaria&rsquo;s person pages and its tags on passages; the biographical dictionaries;
and the earlier full scan of the Bavli, whose every choice was made from our list.</p>

<h2 id="editions">What two editions of the same page really tell us</h2>
<p>I had claimed that a spelling change between editions proves two spellings are one name. You
pointed out that editions often differ because the editors did not agree on who the person was.
So I measured it, on tractate Berakhot, in both Talmuds.</p>
{{GIRSA}}
<p><strong>How to use this.</strong> A difference between editions is a flag on a spot, not an answer. It
says the identity here was open enough that two editors went different ways. Those spots are
worth collecting for two reasons. They are exactly where a careful reader would want a warning.
And they are a ready-made hard test for anything we build.</p>

<h2 id="stages">How we go through them, stage by stage</h2>
<ol class="stages">
<li><h3>Take a dated copy of every edition <span class="tag ok">built</span></h3>
<p>Each piece is saved with its edition, link, date, licence and a fingerprint, so anyone can
re-fetch and confirm they have the same text. Known fault: the same edition with and without
vowel marks is counted as two.</p></li>
<li><h3>Line the editions up with each other <span class="tag bad">not started</span></h3>
<p>Editions cut the text into pieces differently. We match passage to passage so that a name in
one can be compared with the same spot in another.</p></li>
<li><h3>Find everything that might be a name <span class="tag warn">partly built</span></h3>
<p>Be generous here and strict later. Open problems, in order of size: the four bare one-word
names; short forms such as the two-letter ones that can mean five different rabbis; and the
single letters Hebrew glues to the front of words.</p></li>
<li><h3>Sort each distinct string: a person, or not <span class="tag bad">not started</span></h3>
<p>A person, a group such as &ldquo;the Sages&rdquo;, a place, a legal term, a figure from the Bible, or
a title stuck to an ordinary word. This is a judgement, so it goes to the cheap judging model
with three real passages each, once per string, not once per mention. A sample is checked by hand.</p></li>
<li><h3>Find what joins two names <span class="tag warn">partly built</span></h3>
<p>Count the words around every pair of names, before, between and after. My first three patterns
caught {{CAUGHT_PCT}} of {{PAIRS}} pairs, and there are {{PHRASES}} different joining phrases. Counting
finds them all for free. The judging model then labels the rarer ones.</p></li>
<li><h3>Record the clues on each single mention <span class="tag warn">one measured</span></h3>
<p>The wording, the layer of text it sits in, the language of the statement, which body of text
it is from, and the title. See the next section.</p></li>
<li><h3>Build the graph of names <span class="tag warn">prototype</span></h3>
<p>Each name is a point. Each link has a kind, a direction, a weight, and the passages behind it.
The Land of Israel and Babylonia are kept as two separate timelines.</p></li>
<li><h3>Decide which names are one man, and which one name is several <span class="tag bad">not started</span></h3>
<p>The output is a set of guesses at real people, each with odds, plus a pile marked &ldquo;cannot
tell&rdquo;.</p></li>
<li><h3>Put them in order, then on a calendar <span class="tag warn">prototype</span></h3>
<p>Order first, from the strong links. Then pin a few people we agree are certain, and let the
chains carry the rest. Each person gets odds across the eras, not one label.</p></li>
<li><h3>Only now open the biographies <span class="tag bad">not started</span></h3>
<p>For each guessed person, ask of each biography: does its era fit the place the text gives him?
Do the teachers it names match who he actually quotes? It may fit, clash, or there may be no
biography at all.</p></li>
<li><h3>Give every mention its odds <span class="tag bad">not started</span></h3>
<p>Pack up the evidence for a mention and ask the judging model to spread its bets across the
guessed people, with &ldquo;none of these&rdquo; always on offer.</p></li>
<li><h3>Publish, and feed it back into the app <span class="tag bad">not started</span></h3>
<p>The data, the method and the paper. In the app: a rebuilt page index, spelling links that point
at lists, and a reader that shows when it is unsure.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 id="signals">What we pull out of each mention</h2>
<p>Each of these is a clue, never a fact. The last column says whether I have measured it or am
only expecting it to work.</p>
