<tr><td>X is the son of Y</td><td>One generation later. But usually it is just part of one man&rsquo;s name: only about one in six fathers also appears in his own right.</td><td><span class="tag warn">partly</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>The name is in the Mishnah itself</td><td>An early teacher, almost by definition</td><td><span class="tag bad">expected</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>The statement is in Hebrew or in Aramaic</td><td>Early teachers are quoted in Hebrew</td><td><span class="tag bad">expected</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Quoted inside a Mishnah or outside teaching, or in the discussion</td><td>Early or late layer</td><td><span class="tag bad">expected</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Rabbi against Rav</td><td>Land of Israel against Babylonia. Noisy, as you know.</td><td><span class="tag bad">expected</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Which body of text the mention is in</td><td>Place, and roughly time</td><td><span class="tag bad">expected</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>&ldquo;When X came&rdquo; from the Land of Israel</td><td>A traveller, which ties the two timelines together</td><td><span class="tag bad">expected</span></td></tr>
