<h3>The wording clue, which I measured for this review</h3>
<figure>{{FIG_WORD}}</figure>
<p>I took names that have exactly one entry in our list and an era on record, and asked which
wording the Bavli uses for them more often. Every one of the eleven misses goes the same way:
our list says early teacher, the wording says later sage. And they are exactly the names two
men share, one early and one late.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="num">&ldquo;X says&rdquo; (early wording)</th><th class="num">&ldquo;said X&rdquo; (late wording)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>{{MISS_ROWS}}</tbody></table></div>
<p>So this clue does more than date a name. It sorts the single mentions of a shared name into
the early man and the late man, for free, from the text. Rabbi Oshaya is on that list. That is
the pair I tried to split by spelling.</p>

