<p><strong>Two names become one man</strong> when they look alike, keep the same company, and never
appear in the same sentence. A short name following its full form in the same passage is the
commonest case.</p>
<p><strong>One name becomes several men</strong> when its mentions fall into separate groups of
company, or need two places in the order at once, or use the early wording in some places and
the late wording in others.</p>
<p><strong>A biography joins a guessed person</strong> only when its era fits where the text puts him and
its teachers match who he quotes. If it clashes, that is a finding. If there is none, he is a
person we can describe but not name from any reference book, and we say so.</p>

<h2 id="check">How we check it</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A check set for names and links.</strong> About 300 passages, spread across every body of
text and both law and story. A person marks every name, says whether it is a person, and marks
what joins each pair. Two people do a shared part, so we know how often careful readers
disagree. Every stage is then scored as: of what it found, how much was right; and of what was
there, how much it found.</li>
<li><strong>A check set for identity.</strong> About 500 mentions of the hardest shared names, settled by
someone who can read the commentaries. Half is kept locked away and never used while building,
the same discipline as the existing pilot folder.</li>
<li><strong>Sefaria&rsquo;s tags as a second opinion, not the answer.</strong> Where we disagree, a person
reads a sample from both sides: what they tag that the text does not support, and what the text
shows that they leave out. Those two error rates are different and mean different things.</li>
<li><strong>Checks the data can run on itself.</strong> No loops in the order. Nobody named in the Mishnah
quoting a later sage. The two timelines kept apart.</li>
<li><strong>Read before claiming.</strong> Before any number goes in a report, someone reads a random
sample of what is behind it. The 40 I read for this page found a bug that every earlier number
had passed through.</li>
<li><strong>Every bug becomes a test.</strong> The guardian who became a rabbi. Rabbi Meir cut down to
Rabbi. Mar Mai. Rava glued to the next word. Each one is a line in a test file that fails if it
ever comes back.</li>
<li><strong>Do the odds mean what they say?</strong> When the judging model says 90 percent, it should be
right nine times in ten on the locked-away half. We plot that.</li>
<li><strong>Write down what we expect first.</strong> The main measures are fixed before the full run, so
the results cannot quietly choose their own yardstick.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="limits">Where this cannot work</h2>
<p>About {{ALONE}} of mentions in the Bavli sit alone, with no other name and no joining word near
them. Nothing in the text can say who that is. Names that truly appear once cannot be grouped
with anything. For both, the honest outputs are a link to an outside record with its own doubts
attached, or &ldquo;unknown&rdquo;. How big these groups really are is not yet known, because my
count was polluted by strings that are not names.</p>

<hr>
<h2 id="order">The order of work, and what each step buys</h2>
<ol class="stages">
<li><h3>Fix the known bugs and write the tests <span class="tag">me &middot; about a day</span></h3>
<p>Buys: numbers we can stand behind. Nothing below is worth running on a finder that invents
rabbis.</p></li>
<li><h3>Fetch everything, every edition <span class="tag">me &middot; hours of machine time, no cost</span></h3>
<p>Buys: the full text with fingerprints, and a real count of editions per work instead of my spot
checks. The record of what was fetched goes into the repository.</p></li>
<li><h3>Build the two check sets <span class="tag">you, and ideally a scholar &middot; I prepare the sheets</span></h3>
<p>Buys: every later number becomes checkable. This is the step only people can do, and it blocks
every claim of accuracy. It can run alongside steps 4 to 7.</p></li>
<li><h3>Find the names, then sort person from not <span class="tag">me &middot; a few dollars of model calls</span></h3>
<p>Buys: the first honest count of how many name strings there are.</p></li>
<li><h3>Find and label what joins them <span class="tag">me &middot; a few dollars</span></h3>
<p>Buys: the other 85 percent of the evidence that my first patterns missed.</p></li>
<li><h3>Add the clues on each mention <span class="tag">me</span></h3>
<p>Buys: early-or-late for most mentions straight from the wording, before any graph exists.</p></li>
<li><h3>Build the graph; merge and split <span class="tag">me</span></h3>
<p>Buys: the first answer to the headline question, with its doubts stated.</p></li>
<li><h3>Order, then eras <span class="tag">me, after you pick the fixed points</span></h3>
<p>Buys: odds across the eras for everyone the text can place, including people with no entry
anywhere. It also fills many of the {{NOERA}} blank eras in our list.</p></li>
<li><h3>Open the biographies and score them <span class="tag">me, then a person on the clashes</span></h3>
<p>Buys: corrections to the list we ship, and a list of people the reference books do not know.</p></li>
<li><h3>Odds for every mention <span class="tag">me &middot; tens of dollars</span></h3>
<p>Buys: what the reader actually needs on the page.</p></li>
<li><h3>Write it up; rebuild the app&rsquo;s index once <span class="tag">both of us</span></h3>
<p>Buys: the paper, the public data, and a reader that knows what it does not know.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 id="decide">Five things I need you to decide</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Who marks the check sets?</strong> You alone, or can you bring in someone who learns full
time? Two markers is what makes the numbers publishable.</li>
<li><strong>Who are the fixed points?</strong> I would start with a dozen nobody argues about, such as
Rabbi Akiva, Rabbi Yehuda the Prince, Rav, Shmuel, Rabbi Yochanan, Abaye, Rava and Rav Ashi.
You should choose them, not me.</li>
<li><strong>Bavli first, or everything at once?</strong> I lean to fetching everything now, since it is
free, but proving the method on the Bavli and the Mishnah before widening.</li>
<li><strong>The nine live spelling links.</strong> Change them now to point at lists of possible men, or
leave them until the study can do better? I lean to now. It is small and it stops the app
asserting things we no longer believe.</li>
<li><strong>A spending limit for model calls.</strong> The whole plan looks like under a hundred dollars
on the cheap judging model. Say the number you are comfortable with and I will stay under it.</li>
</ol>

